NAME
    web-export - create a standalone offline HTML snapshot of a web page

SYNOPSIS
    web-export <http(s)-url> <export-directory>

DESCRIPTION
    Captures the rendered page with the web-analyze Chromium/CDP recorder,
    including its bounded passive scroll sweep, and writes a local export.
    The export directory must not already exist. Its parent is created when
    needed. The result can be opened directly as index.html; it does not need
    a local HTTP server or network access.

    The exporter uses only resource bodies whose archived size and SHA-256
    match the recorder index. It rewrites captured visual resources to local
    files under assets/. A stylesheet body unavailable from HTTP may use a
    saved CSSOM stylesheet source, identified as cssom-source in manifest.json;
    this is not represented as the original HTTP response body. Non-success
    HTTP errors/redirects, empty non-stylesheet bodies and digest/size
    mismatches are rejected. References without a verified local source are
    removed and counted. Non-HTTP(S) subresource schemes are removed, except
    for embedded data values and document fragments.

    Page scripts, frames, embedded objects, external form submission and
    browser event handlers and hyperlink ping notifications are removed or
    disabled. If the capture contains non-zero document or nested scroll
    offsets, a generated inline helper restores those offsets after load. Its
    SHA-256 content hash is the only script source allowed by the restrictive
    policy; site scripts remain removed. Helper presence is listed in
    manifest.json. The result preserves the rendered DOM, captured scroll position and local visual
    resources; it is an offline snapshot, not a running copy of the site's
    application. Actions that depend on a backend, API, login session, script
    or removed frame do not work offline. Normal hyperlinks remain links to
    their original destinations when clicked.

    The source page and captured assets can contain private content. The
    output directory and temporary capture use restrictive permissions.
    Temporary raw capture data is removed after conversion.

OPERANDS
    http(s)-url       Required absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL.
    export-directory  New destination directory. Existing paths are refused
                      to avoid replacing user data.

OUTPUT
    Prints the absolute path to index.html on stdout. Diagnostics go to stderr.

    export-directory/
      index.html       Rendered offline page with a no-network content policy
      manifest.json    Asset digests, coverage counts and explicit limitations
      assets/          Verified local images, fonts, media and rewritten CSS

EXIT STATUS
    0   Export completed. Check manifest.json for omitted resources.
    1   m_ROOT is missing.
    2   m_LIB_DIR is missing.
    3   Invalid operands.
    4   Invalid URL.
    5   Unsupported URL scheme.
    6   Export parent directory could not be created.
    7   Export destination could not be created or already exists.
    8   Browser capture failed.
    9   Capture directory was not reported.
    10  Resource index is unavailable.
    11  Resource index is invalid.
    12  Rendered DOM is unavailable.
    13  Rendered DOM is empty.
    14  Verified visual assets exceed the 512 MiB limit.
    15  Rendered HTML exceeds the 32 MiB limit.
    16  Chromium could not perform the offline transformation.
    17  Chromium returned no transformed page.
    18  Chromium returned an invalid transformed result.
    19  Private temporary capture data could not be removed.
    20  An unexpected export failure occurred.

ENVIRONMENT AND FILES
    Requires an initialized m environment with Node.js, Chromium and the
    web-analyze command/library installed. Run as `m web-export ...` from a
    clean shell.

    Uses a temporary private web-analyze capture and a temporary isolated
    Chromium profile. Only the export directory is retained. This command
    does not modify the source page, authenticate, click controls, submit
    forms or bypass access challenges.
